{"ts": 1789771334.419206, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've searched the space thoroughly: all 40 singles, a broad set of pairs, and a complete sweep of third features against the best core. The maximum in-sample Sharpe is 0.976 for {+2, \u22126, +31}, and it does not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.019.\n\nNow the key question is what to predict for OOS. I evaluated ~80 specifications and picked the max, so the 0.976 is an upwardly biased estimate \u2014 it contains real signal plus whatever noise happened to favor this combination.\n\nLet me reason about the magnitude of that bias. With n=5000 daily observations, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22... actually for Sharpe estimation the SE is approximately \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/n) in daily units, annualized by \u221a252: about \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22 for modest Sharpes. Selecting the max over ~80 correlated candidates inflates the estimate by something on the order of 1\u20131.5 SE. But these candidates are heavily overlapping (they share feature 2), so the effective number of independent draws is much smaller than 80 \u2014 maybe 10\u201320. That suggests a selection bias of roughly 0.15\u20130.3.\n\nImportantly, the bar itself is informative: it's described as \"search-adjusted\" for the class of specifications I can produce, and it sits at 1.019, above my best. That's the environment telling me that a 0.976 in-sample maximum, found by this much searching, is not distinguishable from what pure overfitting would produce. I should take that seriously rather than treat my number as a genuine edge.\n\nThat said, there is structural evidence of real signal here. Feature 2 alone gives 0.773 \u2014 that's a single pre-specified feature, barely selected at all (it was the top of 40, so mildly biased, but 0.773 is far above the rest of the distribution). The gains from adding features 6 and 31 are modest and consistent with diversification of independent signals rather than noise-fitting. So I don't think this is pure noise; I think it's a real but smaller edge.\n\nMy point estimate should shrink 0.976 substantially. I'll center near 0.70 \u2014 roughly the level of feature 2's standalone signal plus a little genuine diversification benefit, discounting the selection-driven increment. The uncertainty should be wide: OOS could be a regime where these features decay, and my sd should reflect both estimation error and the possibility that more of the in-sample result was overfit than I judge."}
