{"ts": 1789771384.244694, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted\n\n**Specification:** long feature 2, short feature 6, long feature 31 (equal weight)\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 0.976\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.70 \u00b1 0.32\n\n### Search process\n- Evaluated all 40 features individually. Feature 2 was a clear standout (0.773); a second tier sat near 0.35\u20130.48 (features 6 short, 31, 32, 30, 0, 33, 13, 19 short).\n- Tested pairs built on feature 2 \u2014 best reached 0.883.\n- Ran a **complete sweep** of all remaining features as a third leg against the best core {+2, \u22126}, plus triples among the strong second tier. Nothing beat {+2, \u22126, +31} at 0.976.\n\n### On the prediction\nI deliberately predicted well below the in-sample 0.976, for two reasons:\n\n**Selection bias.** I evaluated ~80 specs and reported the maximum. With n=5000 the SE on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.2, and maximizing over candidates \u2014 even correlated ones \u2014 inflates the estimate by something like 0.15\u20130.3. The number I'm reporting is the winner of a search, not an unbiased measurement.\n\n**The bar is evidence.** `status` reported a search-adjusted bar of 1.019 that my 0.976 **does not clear**. That's the environment saying this result isn't distinguishable from what pure overfitting at this search intensity would produce. I treated that as information rather than something to search harder against \u2014 grinding for a combination that squeaks over 1.019 would just be buying more selection bias, not more signal.\n\n**Why not lower.** I don't think it's pure noise. Feature 2 alone scores 0.773 with essentially no selection applied, and it sits far above the rest of the feature distribution. The increments from features 6 and 31 are modest and look like genuine diversification rather than noise-fitting. So I centered near 0.70 \u2014 roughly feature 2's standalone level plus a little real diversification \u2014 and used a wide sd of 0.32 to cover both estimation error and the chance that more of the in-sample result was overfit than I judge.\n\nOne note: at startup a number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Google Drive, Linear, etc.) reported that they need authorization. None were relevant here \u2014 I used only `evaluate`, `status`, and `submit` \u2014 but if you want those available, they'd need to be authorized via your claude.ai connector settings or `claude mcp` in an interactive session."}
